68
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:52.940
My friend Luda, she's Russian.

69
00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.700
She brought this Russian vodka.

70
00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.260
We all got so hammered.

71
00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:58.380
That's a Friendsgiving.

72
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:00.360
It was so fun.

73
00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.060
I actually just found a picture of us recently.

74
00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.520
It came up in my memory.

75
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:05.320
Uh-huh.

76
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:05.840
Yeah.

77
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:06.880
I love that.

78
00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.040
Yeah, we had traded it.

79
00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.840
You know, Josh, he bought me the most insane thing last year for Thanksgiving.

80
00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.700
And he was like, this is going to be a tradition now.

81
00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.080
You're going to have to wear it every Thanksgiving.

82
00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:17.900
I was like, babe.

83
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:19.000
What is it?

84
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:19.340
Okay.

85
00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:20.660
You know, I'm always cold.

86
00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:23.060
And I always want a cozy onesie.

87
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:24.460
I'm like, babe, get me a onesie.

88
00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:25.800
Get me a cozy onesie.

89
00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:26.180
Okay.

90
00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.080
Lady, it's a turkey.

91
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.500
A turkey onesie?

92
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:31.700
First of all, it's a one-size-fits-all.

93
00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:32.980
So it's enormous.

94
00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:34.240
Do you eat in it?

95
00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:35.580
Do you wear it all day?

96
00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.580
No, I can't.

97
00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:39.080
It's like wearing a giant blanket.

98
00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.200
I can't eat in it, but I do put it on.

99
00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.020
Well, I've only done—this is my second year.

100
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:44.500
Okay.

101
00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.500
I don't know what the tradition of this turkey onesie is going to be.

102
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.360
But I told him, here's what I'll do, because he thinks it's so cute.

103
00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:55.580
I put it on, and I watch the Macy's Day Parade in my turkey onesie.

104
00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:56.080
Okay.

105
00:03:56.660 --> 00:03:57.620
And then, you know—

106
00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:58.280
That sounds cozy.

107
00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:02.220
We get the food prep going, but I don't wear it to the actual Thanksgiving dinner.

108
00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.640
What is it that Josh thinks is so funny?

109
00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:08.680
I look like a tiny, tiny turkey.

110
00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.360
Do you watch football?

111
00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:11.800
Oh, yeah.

112
00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:12.900
Football's always on.

113
00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:18.280
So when I got into football, I was dating a guy who was from Detroit.

114
00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.600
And you know Detroit plays every Thanksgiving.

115
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:21.800
And the Cowboys.

116
00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:22.420
Yes.

117
00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.180
Now I'm married to a Dallas man.

118
00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:24.460
Yeah.

119
00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:25.960
Now I'm a Cowboys fan.

120
00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:26.400
Oh, yeah.

121
00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:27.060
Yeah.

122
00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.320
So Cowboys, every Thanksgiving, you've got to work the meal around it.

123
00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:32.860
Half of my family's from Texas.

124
00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:33.240
Lady.

125
00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:34.040
It's always—

126
00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:34.480
Yes, you know.

127
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:35.320
What am I saying?

128
00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:35.920
I know.

129
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:36.600
Of course.

130
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:40.720
Well, listen, we have a really exciting show for you today.

131
00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.880
I don't even know what to say about it.

132
00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:46.920
It's maybe one of my favorite episodes we've ever done.

133
00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:50.540
Jenna and I are in love with this interview, you guys.

134
00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:51.180
I'm sorry.

135
00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.480
It's an interview, but it was so wonderful.

136
00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:54.640
Well, Jenna, you tell everyone.

137
00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.560
Well, we got to sit down with Zach Woods.

138
00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:00.980
As you know, Zach played Gabe Lewis on 52 episodes of The Office.

139
00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.380
He was on from season six to season nine.

140
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:05.420
We talk all about it.

141
00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:09.360
We talk about his entire time on the show and other things.

142
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:10.240
Here's the thing.

143
00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:14.020
He's just so interesting, and he's so smart and funny.

144
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:19.280
And we would be talking about the show, and then he would throw you and I like a curveball question.

145
00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:25.640
Like a deep, light question about what it means to be just human and alive.

146
00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:26.020
Yes.

147
00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:27.020
Like, so fun.

148
00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.280
Oh, I just can't wait for everyone to listen to this interview.

149
00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:31.320
I just love it.

150
00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:37.520
Well, you guys, Zach is currently working on a new stop-motion animation series called In the Know.

151
00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:40.940
He's the co-creator, the director, and star of the show.

152
00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:45.220
And Greg Daniels is one of the executive producers, so you know this is going to be good.

153
00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:48.800
The show premieres on Peacock in the new year, so be on the lookout.

154
00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.080
Yeah, we'll share about it in stories.

155
00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:51.980
But you know what?

156
00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:56.520
I think we take a quick break and get to this interview because it's so fantastic.

157
00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:57.500
You're going to love it.

158
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.100
And the second half, dare I say, is even better than the firSt.

159
00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:02.740
It keeps getting better and better.

160
00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:03.960
Here it is.

161
00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:16.100
Hello.

162
00:06:16.540 --> 00:06:18.440
Zach, how wonderful to see you.

163
00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:20.440
How wonderful to see you and you.

164
00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.080
Should we tell people we've actually been chatting off mic for like 30 minutes?

165
00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:28.720
Oh, seriously, we've been talking for half an hour, but we decided we should probably get to the podcaSt.

166
00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:29.380
Yes.

167
00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.560
They literally said we're going to do a big fake hello, and then you immediately told on yourself.

168
00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.140
You're giving away our secrets.

169
00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:35.640
Sorry.

170
00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:41.480
I'm going to do an Office Man, Office Ladies podcast, where I tell the stories behind the Office Ladies podcaSt.

171
00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:41.980
Okay.

172
00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:42.440
Oh.

173
00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:43.740
It'll be like Russian dolls.

174
00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:44.520
Oh, my gosh.

175
00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:47.800
So you listen to Office Ladies, and then you break down our podcaSt.

176
00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:51.600
Right, and I'll have you guys on to talk about the podcast when you're talking about the...

177
00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.840
This might be a genius idea.

178
00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:01.380
Well, we're going to kick things off with the question we ask all of our guests to start, which is, how did you get your job on The Office?

179
00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:03.060
It's a good question.

180
00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:08.820
Alison Jones, who you guys both know, was like a fairy godmother to me.

181
00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:13.940
Like, she, for people who, I mean, I know you've talked about her on the show, but she was the casting director for the show in, like,

182
00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.860
basically American comedy for a decade or more, right?

183
00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:24.920
And she brought me in to her office, which was in Gower Studios, where I guess they used to shoot I Love Lucy and stuff.

184
00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:25.460
Wow.

185
00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:26.240
Right?

186
00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:26.780
Isn't that right?

187
00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:27.640
I think so, yeah.

188
00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:30.080
It was like one of these old Hollywood things.

189
00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.000
It's got, like, plaques everywhere.

190
00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:32.400
Right.

191
00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:32.700
Yeah.

192
00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:33.960
And, like, plastered.

193
00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.960
It just feels like, you know, whatever.

194
00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:43.640
You can, like, it's like where you can imagine, like, Clark Gable stumbling into there after he, like, killed someone and be, like, telling his publicist, like, bury it.

195
00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:45.040
But anyway.

196
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:47.820
Poor Clark Gable.

197
00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:48.820
Sorry, Clark Gable.

198
00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:50.040
You probably never killed anyone.

199
00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:50.880
I'm sorry.

200
00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:56.080
I just feel like, you know, like, old Hollywood stars were always killing people and telling their publicist to cover it up.

201
00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:56.260
To bury it.

202
00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:56.940
Get rid of it.

203
00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:57.220
Yeah.

204
00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.640
She was just some girl no one's going to know.

205
00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:01.200
Yeah.

206
00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:01.480
Anyway.

207
00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.060
But got distracted.

208
00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.160
Allison Jones.

209
00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:04.480
Allison Jones.

210
00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:04.720
Gower.

211
00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:12.480
So I remember meeting with her, and I'd done this movie called In the Loop, and she'd seen it, and she brought me in for a meeting,

212
00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.900
and I really hadn't done anything except for this one little independent movie.

213
00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:21.820
And she talked to me for, like, an hour, and then at the end of it, she just went, I'm going to help you.

214
00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:26.360
Which is such a wild thing for someone to say in Hollywood, you know.

215
00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:30.000
And then even a wilder thing to mean and follow through on.

216
00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:39.140
And then she got me a meeting with Greg, Daniels, and Paul, and they just gave me a part on The Office, which is a show that I had been, like, obsessively watching.

217
00:08:39.160 --> 00:08:40.580
I felt like I won a conteSt.

218
00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:41.960
Like, you know what I mean?

219
00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:45.680
Like, you win a raffle, and you can do, like, a walk-on on, like, a TV show.

220
00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:51.620
Like, I felt like it was so bizarre to me that she almost, like, to a suspicious degree where it's, like.

221
00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.400
Like, who's going to jump out and say, gotcha?

222
00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:53.900
Right.

223
00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:54.240
Yeah.

224
00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:59.700
Or is she going to, like, am I going to get a call in here where Allison Jones is, like, throw napalm in one of my enemy's faces?

225
00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:01.680
It's, like, time for payback.

226
00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:02.540
Now, yeah, yeah.

227
00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:03.080
Yeah, exactly.

228
00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:06.720
And then, yeah, and then I just got this part.

229
00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:14.560
And then I moved into this house in Echo Park where it was a basement, and I'm tall, and there were these rafters.

230
00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:17.640
So I had to constantly duck, like, I couldn't.

231
00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:22.300
It was the ceiling was too low, so I was constantly doing these, like, kind of body rolls around my living room.

232
00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:30.060
And I was, somehow she got wind of this, and she was like, oh, I have an empty condo in Beachwood Canyon, which I'll give to you for a song, basically.

233
00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:35.840
And then she gave me this, like, beautiful condo to live in for, like, very low rent where they used to house.

234
00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:41.480
It was, like, a dormitory for starlets back in the, like, it was, like, the MGM gals would be there.

235
00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:48.620
And in the basement, there was a beauty salon where they had to go through the works before they could go out into the world because they didn't want anyone seeing, like,

236
00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:51.340
the MGM girls looking less than their best, basically.

237
00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:51.820
Wow.

238
00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:53.380
So I was effectively an MGM girl.

239
00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:53.740
Yeah.

240
00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:54.560
Thanks to Allison Jones.

241
00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:55.660
But I'm being long-winded.

242
00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:57.880
I'm a little, like, we're a little nervous because we're starting to be all.

243
00:09:57.940 --> 00:09:58.160
No, no, no.

244
00:09:58.160 --> 00:09:59.980
I'll be less long-winded, but I will just say.

245
00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:09.340
Allison Jones, I will never be able to adequately express the difference that Allison Jones made in my life professionally, residentially, personally.

246
00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:16.540
Just, like, that woman just, like, tapped me with her magic wand and gave me my life as a, you know, in L.A., basically.

247
00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:18.780
I mean, we feel the same way about Allison Jones.

248
00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:19.120
Yeah.

249
00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:23.920
I had a general meeting with her, and she sort of did the same thing.

250
00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:26.080
It was like one-hour meeting.

251
00:10:26.520 --> 00:10:27.920
Hey, I like you.

252
00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.260
I think you're talented.

253
00:10:29.460 --> 00:10:30.320
I'm going to help you out.

254
00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:34.960
And then just called me in for years for little bit parts.

255
00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:40.520
And then eventually, how lucky am I that the person who decided to help me was hired to cast The Office?

256
00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:42.560
That was just my good luck.

257
00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:43.180
Yeah.

258
00:10:43.480 --> 00:10:43.820
You know?

259
00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:47.800
Because then she was like, well, I'm going to have you meet on this new show, The Office.

260
00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:49.480
So it was the same thing.

261
00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:54.660
Like, and I've heard people tell this story about Allison Jones many, many times.

262
00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:55.880
It's crazy.

263
00:10:56.240 --> 00:11:05.180
And she has, like, this kind of, like, flinty New Englander quality where if you try to tell this to her face, it's just like, it's like shining light on a vampire or something.

264
00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:05.820
She juSt.

265
00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:07.800
She refuses the accolades.

266
00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:08.160
That's right.

267
00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:08.600
Yeah.

268
00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:09.120
That's right.

269
00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:09.860
She can't tolerate.

270
00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:15.360
It's just like, that's been my experience anyway, where you try to, like, look her in the eyes and be like, thank you.

271
00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:15.680
Yeah.

272
00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:16.560
She's like.

273
00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:18.040
No, it's fine.

274
00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:19.840
Here, have a homemade cookie I just made.

275
00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:22.400
Because she always has, like, delicious cookies.

276
00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:23.020
Right.

277
00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:27.800
She just feels like that awesome, warm aunt that's going to look out for you.

278
00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:28.580
It's true.

279
00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:29.680
Allison, we love you.

280
00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:30.620
I love you.

281
00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:32.500
FYI, if you haven't gathered it.

282
00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:39.440
Besides In the Loop, which I remember in which everyone was talking about when you came to work with us, it's a great movie.

283
00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:41.840
Besides that, what was your background?

284
00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:43.720
Like, do you have an improv background?

285
00:11:44.140 --> 00:11:48.140
Yeah, I started doing improv at Upright Citizens Brigade in New York.

286
00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:50.080
And I mostly just thought I was going to do that.

287
00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:55.340
I remember talking to someone and being like, I just want to get a temp job and do improv at night.

288
00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.040
That was eight years of my life.

289
00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:58.180
It's fun, right?

290
00:11:58.300 --> 00:11:58.460
Yeah.

291
00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:03.820
Did you feel like when you were doing that, did it feel unsatisfying in some fundamental way?

292
00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:05.060
No, it was the most fun.

293
00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:05.400
Right.

294
00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:06.220
It was the most fun.

295
00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:07.860
Because the job I didn't really care about.

296
00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:10.480
I just needed to pay bills so I could go do improv.

297
00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:12.840
And then you're with, like, you have a community.

298
00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:14.860
Yeah, and they all are like-minded.

299
00:12:15.240 --> 00:12:17.000
And we were so into it.

300
00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.380
We were probably really obnoxious.

301
00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:23.960
Well, I used to teach, too, when I was in New York to help, like, I liked teaching and also it helped me survive.

302
00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:31.640
But I remember once being in a cab in New York and going past an improv theater and seeing all these adults outside.

303
00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:36.400
And they were, like, throwing, like, imaginary knives to each other or whatever.

304
00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:37.180
They were doing some, like, warm-up.

305
00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:37.980
Some improv game.

306
00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:43.480
It was so humiliating and so, you could not look nerdier and less cool.

307
00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:46.500
And these people, I just felt my heart swell in my cheSt.

308
00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:51.120
Like, seeing these, like, grown adults just, like, kind of, they looked so happy.

309
00:12:51.400 --> 00:12:58.100
They were just, like, being so cringed so happily out in public in New York on the street.

310
00:12:58.100 --> 00:12:59.680
I was just like, God bless you guys.

311
00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:00.880
Like, it's so sweet.

312
00:13:01.100 --> 00:13:02.820
Like, I found it so moving.

313
00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:04.580
You're like, I want in.

314
00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:09.820
I thought it was so perfect when they made the choice to have Michael Scott in an improv group.

315
00:13:10.100 --> 00:13:10.940
It's so perfect.

316
00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:12.120
It was so perfect.

317
00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:14.600
Like, I took improv classes with a Michael Scott.

318
00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:15.120
Oh, my God.

319
00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:16.200
It's mostly Michael Scott.

320
00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:16.340
Yeah.

321
00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:17.500
Oh, my gosh.

322
00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:19.940
How much were you told about the character Gabe?

323
00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:22.360
Did you know it was going to be, like, a long arc?

324
00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:23.500
No.

325
00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:25.180
I didn't know much.

326
00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:31.220
It's a pretty foggy memory because I think because it felt like such a, I felt like such

327
00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:34.740
a raffle winner where it's like you can be on your favorite show.

328
00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:42.300
It created, like, such a rush of anxiety that, like, I've never experienced anything like it

329
00:13:42.300 --> 00:13:42.820
before since.

330
00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:50.200
Like, to be, like, completely inexperienced as a TV actor and to be thrust into a show

331
00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:51.460
where you're like, you know what I mean?

332
00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:53.340
It's just, like, it was really overwhelming.

333
00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:59.720
And I remember, like, when I first moved out here, right before I started filming, I

334
00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:01.300
remember there was, like, some moment.

335
00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:03.980
This is a really crazy compromising moment.

336
00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:05.480
I was at Walmart.

337
00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:08.480
First of all, I didn't know how to drive because I'd been living in New York.

338
00:14:08.860 --> 00:14:13.400
So I had to, like, as an adult man, go to Pennsylvania and have my father, like, reteach

339
00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:15.240
me to drive because I was like, I'm going to be in LA.

340
00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:16.840
You have to teach me to drive.

341
00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:21.200
So my dad taught me to drive, like, some weird, like, little short film or something like.

342
00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:27.820
And then I remember when we started shooting, driving terrified me so much that I would show

343
00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:32.700
up on set and my jaw would be so clenched that I had a hard time saying words.

344
00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:39.040
So I would take CDs, Frank Sinatra CDs, and I would put them in the car and force myself

345
00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:44.080
to sing along with old blue eyes so that my jaw would stay open so that I wouldn't show

346
00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:46.540
up with, like, rigor mortis face.

347
00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:53.500
And then I think before I'd even shot my first scene, being in a Walmart, buying, like, multiple

348
00:14:54.220 --> 00:14:58.720
humidifiers and lozenges and shit because I was like, I'm going to lose my voice and

349
00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:00.120
then I'm not going to be able to do it.

350
00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:03.740
And I'm going to, I was so panicked and I was worried I was going to forget my lines.

351
00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:05.900
So I was, like, muttering my lines in a Walmart.

352
00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:07.380
For some reason, I didn't get a basket.

353
00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:12.660
So I just had, like, armfuls of, like, humidifiers and lozenges just, like, muttering, like, my

354
00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:14.580
lines to myself like a crazy person.

355
00:15:14.820 --> 00:15:20.060
So what I really remember from that time is just the white-hot panic of, like, you know,

356
00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:20.280
like.

357
00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:22.540
Were you prone to losing your voice?

358
00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.420
What was this anxiety about losing your voice?

359
00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:26.600
I was prone to losing my mind.

360
00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:27.200
I was, like.

361
00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:28.180
Put you in the Walmart.

362
00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:30.140
I think it was just, like, anything.

363
00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:31.640
You know, you have, like, free-ranging.

364
00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:35.480
Sometimes I feel like anxiety is just, like, a giant, like, military helicopter that needs

365
00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:36.520
some place to land.

366
00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:37.240
You know what I mean?

367
00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:41.320
And it was, like, oh, today it'll be landing on the landing pad of, like, vocal anxiety.

368
00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:45.860
Tomorrow it'll be, like, whatever that I'll, you know, some weird hypochondriacal thing

369
00:15:45.860 --> 00:15:46.780
in a different direction.

370
00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:47.600
Yes, yes.

371
00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:51.940
So I don't remember what they told me, but I didn't think I was, I thought it might just

372
00:15:51.940 --> 00:15:55.800
be a few episodes and then they gave me more and I was, like, wow, that's great.

373
00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:57.920
Do you remember your first day on set?

374
00:15:58.340 --> 00:15:58.660
Yeah.

375
00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:01.020
What did you shoot your very first day?

376
00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:04.780
I think the first thing I shot was a, to camera, like, one of the interviews.

377
00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:05.680
A talking head.

378
00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:10.400
That's a good way to start, actually, because it's just you.

379
00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:13.020
No one is dependent on your line for timing.

380
00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:14.440
You're not driving a scene.

381
00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:14.780
Right.

382
00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.020
So I think that's actually a really smart way to start.

383
00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:18.660
They might have done that on purpose, too.

384
00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:21.260
What was your first impression coming on the set?

385
00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:24.700
Well, John Krasinski directed the episode that I was doing.

386
00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:29.280
And I remember something happened on set and it, like, took longer than they were expecting.

387
00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.100
So I was sort of in my trailer just churning.

388
00:16:31.740 --> 00:16:32.820
And then they called me to set.

389
00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:35.400
And I think John knew that I was kind of, like, pent up and terrified.

390
00:16:35.780 --> 00:16:39.120
And so he just, like, even though I'm sure it had been a long day and there was a lot

391
00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:43.600
left to shoot, he, like, let me just improvise a lot in the talking head.

392
00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:46.920
And I doubt they used any of it, but it just was, like, it let me relax.

393
00:16:47.060 --> 00:16:49.220
And he was so kind about it.

394
00:16:49.760 --> 00:16:54.280
The big thing that I remember, and part of why I was, like, excited to do this is because,

395
00:16:54.380 --> 00:16:57.140
like, I'm not good at staying in touch with people and stuff.

396
00:16:57.140 --> 00:17:03.140
But I feel, yeah, that I feel like is important to me to communicate to people who listen to

397
00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:11.820
this, which is the level of kindness and hospitality that was extended to me on that show was bizarre.

398
00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:16.920
Like, if you're in the late seasons of a TV show that has, like, such a deep bench of

399
00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:22.120
amazing characters and some new person shows up who's going to suck up even more oxygen,

400
00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:24.420
eat up more story time, you know what I mean?

401
00:17:24.420 --> 00:17:28.920
And it would be very easy to understand if people were not all that receptive to that,

402
00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:32.740
especially if it's someone who's, like, a rookie who's still just, like, learning about, like,

403
00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:34.260
okay, what's an eyeline?

404
00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:36.180
How do I, like, what's a mark?

405
00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:36.760
You know what I mean?

406
00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:41.020
Like, stuff, like, just very rookie stuff that I'm having to learn on the go.

407
00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:47.200
Like, it would be totally reasonable if people had a kind of professional detachment from that

408
00:17:47.200 --> 00:17:47.600
person.

409
00:17:48.420 --> 00:17:51.840
But everyone was so ostentatiously kind to me.

410
00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:53.120
Ostentatious makes it sound bad.

411
00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:55.120
Just generous, kind.

412
00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:57.480
Like, you guys were so lovely to me.

413
00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:59.580
You guys were so sweet to me and inclusive.

414
00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:04.800
I remember, like, I would just sort of camp out at, like, Phyllis and Leslie's desk and

415
00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:08.980
watch them, like, shop for, they were both, like, decorating their houses and they were, like.

416
00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:12.620
Yes, she was looking for a gate for, like, I think two years.

417
00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:13.680
She wanted a new.

418
00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:13.920
More, maybe.

419
00:18:14.140 --> 00:18:14.260
Yeah.

420
00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:16.420
We looked at every gate possible.

421
00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:20.100
I just remember basically, yeah, like, gate shopping with Leslie and Phyllis.

422
00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:23.040
And, like, they would show me and they would be so nice and joke around with me.

423
00:18:23.100 --> 00:18:25.820
I remember Kate Flannery went, really went out of her way.

424
00:18:26.360 --> 00:18:30.400
Oscar, all the, and not just in a kind of, again, not just in a perfunctory, like, professional

425
00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:30.600
way.

426
00:18:30.680 --> 00:18:32.340
They'd invite me to their houses.

427
00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:34.740
You guys would include me in, like, social stuff.

428
00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:39.460
I just, it was so, it was, like, the photo negative of what happened to the character

429
00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:40.140
Gabe, right?

430
00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:45.560
Like, Gabe is, like, this, like, unfortunate creepy guy shows up and is immediately and utterly

431
00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:46.160
ostracized.

432
00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.460
It was, like, yeah, it was the exact opposite where I was, like, oh, my God, look at this.

433
00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:53.380
Like, this group of people is just, like, so envelopingly sweet.

434
00:18:53.380 --> 00:18:58.640
So, that was life-changing because I was already so fragile and terrified because it was such

435
00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:05.160
a new experience and I wanted so desperately to do a good job and to be met with that kind

436
00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:10.700
of generosity of spirit was, I think, formative for me.

437
00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:12.720
I mean, we loved the character Gabe.

438
00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:17.080
I mean, in re-watching, like, Gabe has made us laugh so hard.

439
00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:21.920
But one of the things we've been most excited about doing this podcast is being able to

440
00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:26.260
reconnect with these people that were such an amazing chapter of our life.

441
00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:28.760
And you're one of those people, we've talked about you so much.

442
00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:30.160
We're so happy you're here.

443
00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:36.440
I mean, we still talked about the gift you brought to my Yankee Swap Christmas party,

444
00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:40.560
which was a sarcophagus, like, jewelry box.

445
00:19:41.140 --> 00:19:44.540
It was the gift that everyone wanted.

446
00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:47.080
I mean, legend, gift.

447
00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:47.860
Thank you.

448
00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:52.300
I mean, that's, like, white elephant stuff is, like, a real source of anxiety, right?

449
00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:55.680
Because it's, like, it feels like a moral, spiritual teSt.

450
00:19:56.260 --> 00:19:57.460
So I'm glad that I passed.

451
00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:00.720
You passed, and I have the sarcophagus.

452
00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:01.420
You do?

453
00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:02.540
Yes, it's mine.

454
00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:04.220
Yeah, I went home with it.

455
00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:05.800
Yeah, I have it on my bookshelf.

456
00:20:05.860 --> 00:20:07.080
And it's not cursed or anything, right?

457
00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:08.320
Doesn't seem like it.

458
00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:09.000
Okay, cool.

459
00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:10.440
Yeah, it seems like a dead you.

460
00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:13.900
I hadn't actively pursued a curse, but if there's a small sarcophagus,

461
00:20:14.040 --> 00:20:17.140
the odds that it has some sort of curse feels pretty high.

462
00:20:17.320 --> 00:20:18.920
It's been good so far.

463
00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:20.460
It's doing great for you.

464
00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:20.960
It is.

465
00:20:20.960 --> 00:20:23.080
It was a heated battle that you won.

466
00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:28.360
I also, off topic here, but in anticipation of talking to you today,

467
00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:30.120
I sort of went back through old emails,

