00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:01.160
Yes, I know him.

563
00:24:01.300 --> 00:24:02.180
Yes, wonderful guy.

564
00:24:02.180 --> 00:24:03.580
We should be best friends now.

565
00:24:03.860 --> 00:24:05.340
Well, that was my next line.

566
00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:07.620
So we are clearly best friends then.

567
00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:08.900
Same hair coloriSt.

568
00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:10.000
So much in common.

569
00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:11.540
I'm your emergency contact.

570
00:24:11.740 --> 00:24:12.400
Yeah, exactly.

571
00:24:12.400 --> 00:24:17.020
Let me ask you, so because for many people, you are that, like, in other words, when people

572
00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:21.940
see you, right, it generates the same feelings in them that Kathy Bage generated in you.

573
00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.000
So what is the version?

574
00:24:23.320 --> 00:24:27.160
Like when people come up to you guys and are like, actually, did you know that we shared

575
00:24:27.160 --> 00:24:28.420
a, you know, whatever?

576
00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:30.800
We stay in the same summer camp cabin?

577
00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:34.440
Like, does it feel alienating to you when people do that?

578
00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:35.300
Or does it feel nice?

579
00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:36.500
Or does it just depend on the person?

580
00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:39.540
Like, well, I guess what I'm asking is like, or maybe is it okay to ask you guys?

581
00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:48.640
What is the version of being approached in a situation like that, where maybe it's not just

582
00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:54.660
like in the airport, but it's like a situation that has some containment that feels good to

583
00:24:54.660 --> 00:24:58.840
you versus the one that feels like, like if you're Kathy Bates, how do you want someone

584
00:24:58.840 --> 00:24:59.480
to approach you?

585
00:24:59.640 --> 00:25:03.820
Well, I'll say that I just did the Mean Girls movie musical.

586
00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:05.240
I play the mom.

587
00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:05.720
Yay!

588
00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:12.980
And after I got on the set and I like was working, I called Angela and I was like, Angela,

589
00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:23.200
we are now like the old lady, like the people, like these kids and the crew and stuff, they're

590
00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:29.980
treating me like the way we were around like Kathy Bates or around some of these like people

591
00:25:29.980 --> 00:25:32.840
who had like the resumes and all that kind of stuff.

592
00:25:32.840 --> 00:25:40.700
I'm like, it's so weird and it feels like it was so bizarre because I was like, oh, I'm like

593
00:25:40.700 --> 00:25:44.480
not at all worthy of your, what do you call it?

594
00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:50.640
Like they're just giving me like, like, yes, like sort of like, we thank you so much for

595
00:25:50.640 --> 00:25:54.360
doing our film and the directors were just like, we can't believe we got you.

596
00:25:54.480 --> 00:25:55.760
And I'm like, you can't?

597
00:25:55.760 --> 00:25:58.440
I'm like, it's the Mean Girls movie musical.

598
00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:00.560
I feel like people are lining up to do this.

599
00:26:00.720 --> 00:26:03.520
You're being so overly kind to me.

600
00:26:03.640 --> 00:26:05.180
But I do remember that.

601
00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:09.800
Like when we would be at an award show, like someone from a television show that we had

602
00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:14.500
watched, you get a chance to meet them or they give you an award or something.

603
00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:17.080
You're like, oh my God, I can't believe this person.

604
00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:19.460
I was like, we're the old timers now.

605
00:26:19.700 --> 00:26:20.000
Yeah.

606
00:26:20.140 --> 00:26:22.680
We're the old timers who have been in the biz for a while.

607
00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:25.500
And I actually thought it was really cool.

608
00:26:25.820 --> 00:26:33.580
I'm still very humbled by it all that, you know, that I got to stop temping and got to,

609
00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:41.220
you know, take the skills I learned from my improv classes and get paid to do something

610
00:26:41.220 --> 00:26:41.640
I love.

611
00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:47.200
It's still very humbling to me that people watch the show, enjoy the show, want to watch me

612
00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:48.900
be silly or whatever it is.

613
00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:51.100
You know, I always feel like I won the lottery.

614
00:26:51.100 --> 00:26:52.820
So I'm still taken aback by it.

615
00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:55.660
Like what you're saying, it just, I don't think that'll ever go away.

616
00:26:55.840 --> 00:27:00.240
I'm still, I still can't believe I, I got to do this, you know?

617
00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:02.080
I wonder if Kathy Bates had that feeling.

618
00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:02.920
Yeah, maybe.

619
00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:07.560
I will tell you, here's an example of when maybe it's not fun or appropriate.

620
00:27:08.380 --> 00:27:13.760
I, towards the end of my pregnancy was having some pain and they said, okay, let's get you

621
00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:18.420
to the specialist for this special kind of sonogram where they can make sure everything's

622
00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:18.700
okay.

623
00:27:20.980 --> 00:27:26.580
So I met with this person I've never met before and he's conducting the sonogram, you

624
00:27:26.580 --> 00:27:30.980
know, I'm just like laying there and he's like, so is Dwight going to be stopping by?

625
00:27:31.300 --> 00:27:31.880
Oh boy.

626
00:27:32.160 --> 00:27:32.380
Uh-huh.

627
00:27:32.380 --> 00:27:37.360
And I was like, you mean a fictional character I'm in a television show with that is not

628
00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:38.380
the father of my baby?

629
00:27:38.640 --> 00:27:38.880
No.

630
00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:40.420
What?

631
00:27:41.140 --> 00:27:44.280
Like, I'm coming to you because I have pain in my pelvis.

632
00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:45.040
That's so horrible.

633
00:27:45.060 --> 00:27:50.760
And you're giving me a sonogram and I'm laying here feeling so exposed and you asked me,

634
00:27:50.920 --> 00:27:54.100
I knew he was like, and he had been like 20 minutes.

635
00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:58.140
So he'd been holding on to that and then he was just like, when is Dwight stopping by?

636
00:27:58.140 --> 00:28:00.960
So maybe, maybe here's a tip.

637
00:28:01.460 --> 00:28:05.700
Like, if you're in that situation, maybe don't bring up the TV show.

638
00:28:05.940 --> 00:28:12.020
I feel like medical professionals in particular should never bring up your celebrity in an

639
00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:12.640
exam room.

640
00:28:13.160 --> 00:28:14.440
Well, I think that's fair.

641
00:28:14.900 --> 00:28:15.780
I think it's fair to you.

642
00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:18.180
That's just a good, solid boundary.

643
00:28:19.060 --> 00:28:19.300
Yeah.

644
00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:22.980
So you're probably there because you're worried about something or concerned.

645
00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:23.280
Yes, that's right.

646
00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:24.180
And that's right.

647
00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:24.420
Yeah.

648
00:28:24.660 --> 00:28:25.060
You know what?

649
00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:26.000
Tell me in the lobby.

650
00:28:26.000 --> 00:28:27.480
Tell me in the lobby afterwards.

651
00:28:27.480 --> 00:28:28.900
You need to be you, right?

652
00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:32.220
You need to be who you are and know that that person sees you.

653
00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:37.260
You know, I think an analogy that I think of all the time is like, I'm an adult person

654
00:28:37.260 --> 00:28:41.980
and I pay a mortgage and I'm raising two children and all this stuff.

655
00:28:42.120 --> 00:28:50.080
And yet, all the time, I feel like a little bit of like, I can't believe that I am tasked

656
00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:51.780
with these adult responsibilities.

657
00:28:51.780 --> 00:28:57.920
Like, I can't like young people will look at me and I know that I look like an old person

658
00:28:57.920 --> 00:28:58.300
to them.

659
00:28:58.400 --> 00:29:04.620
But like inside, I still kind of feel like a flailing 23 year old who doesn't know what

660
00:29:04.620 --> 00:29:05.020
they're doing.

661
00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:07.400
So I feel like that's part of it, too.

662
00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:12.080
Like when people are approaching me and they're like, oh, my God, I love your body of work

663
00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:12.960
and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

664
00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:20.040
I still feel like the struggling actor who's trying to make it like that's never left me.

665
00:29:20.340 --> 00:29:26.440
I will say like before I was on the show, that blog post you wrote to actors.

666
00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:29.280
Yeah, that I turned into a book later.

667
00:29:29.480 --> 00:29:29.580
Yeah.

668
00:29:29.900 --> 00:29:31.840
But at that point, I think it was just available.

669
00:29:32.060 --> 00:29:32.940
It was just a blog poSt.

670
00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:33.300
Yeah.

671
00:29:33.760 --> 00:29:34.760
Advice to actors.

672
00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:39.500
It was so because I'd read that a lot multiple times before ever meeting you or being on the

673
00:29:39.500 --> 00:29:39.660
show.

674
00:29:39.660 --> 00:29:41.800
Oh, my gosh, I didn't even know that, Zach.

675
00:29:42.040 --> 00:29:43.660
Yeah, it was so orienting.

676
00:29:43.780 --> 00:29:48.840
I think because a lot of times I think when people can feel the distance between how they're

677
00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:54.560
perceived and how and their interior sense of themselves we're describing, I think sometimes

678
00:29:54.560 --> 00:30:00.260
people's response to that is to try to identify publicly only with the way they're perceived

679
00:30:00.260 --> 00:30:00.760
or something.

680
00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:07.820
But I remember for you at that time, to share the flailing, to share the kind of blemishy,

681
00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:17.100
angsty, unsure kind of experience that you'd had was so helpful because it's so isolating to aspire to

682
00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:17.300
something.

683
00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:18.740
Well, I don't know if that's even true.

684
00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:21.660
It's scary to – what am I trying to say?

685
00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:24.940
There's a Cherry Jones – you know Cherry Jones, the actor Cherry Jones?

686
00:30:25.460 --> 00:30:29.060
She has this quote that I love so much where she says, theater is where we comfort each

687
00:30:29.060 --> 00:30:32.980
other with our shortcomings, whereas I'll show you mine and you'll see yours reflected

688
00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:35.340
and then we'll have a night of laughs and catharsis and whatever.

689
00:30:35.740 --> 00:30:39.700
In a way, when you wrote that thing, it's like – not that that's shortcomings exactly,

690
00:30:39.740 --> 00:30:43.560
but I felt comforted by your vulnerability and by your ownership of that vulnerability

691
00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:45.540
because I felt so vulnerable.

692
00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:52.980
So seeing like, oh wow, someone who I admire, who has like an exciting career and who is willing

693
00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:59.240
to be introspective and revealing in this way and expose the gap between public perception

694
00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:03.240
and interior experience, like that for me was really helpful.

695
00:31:03.520 --> 00:31:05.320
Sorry, that's such a long-winded answer.

696
00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:06.600
I loved every word of that.

697
00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:12.120
No, and I think it's your question too, which is like, we were so in awe of Kathy Bates,

698
00:31:12.260 --> 00:31:18.660
but like, maybe Kathy Bates just feels like the same tripping over her own feet actor that

699
00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:22.460
we all feel like when we first start a role or first start a job or whatever, but we never

700
00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:26.680
asked because we just assumed she must be filled with all this confidence and all of

701
00:31:26.680 --> 00:31:27.540
this, all of it.

702
00:31:27.660 --> 00:31:29.260
But that also goes back to you, Zach.

703
00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:33.100
You talk about all this anxiety and you're holding these things in Walmart and you can't

704
00:31:33.100 --> 00:31:33.900
believe you're on the show.

705
00:31:34.420 --> 00:31:39.540
So you were seamlessly a part of our show from the very beginning.

706
00:31:40.100 --> 00:31:46.540
Like, it's kind of blowing my mind that you're telling me that you were anxious or that you

707
00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:51.360
even were inexperienced because I knew you had done In the Loop, but I assumed like there

708
00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:52.440
were all these other things too.

709
00:31:52.600 --> 00:31:56.460
I had no, I really had no idea that The Office was your first television job.

710
00:31:56.720 --> 00:32:01.400
Like, and for how just amazing you were, right?

711
00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:05.440
So just amazing right out of the gate and such a smart improviser.

712
00:32:05.580 --> 00:32:09.420
I know I'm going to be an improv nerd here, but I just love it when people are really smart

713
00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:14.880
and say really witty, smart things and don't go for the obvious joke and don't go for too

714
00:32:14.880 --> 00:32:17.340
much and are understated and you're all of those things.

715
00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:20.860
And I have loved watching your scenes so much.

716
00:32:20.860 --> 00:32:26.220
And I love going to the script and reading the script and then watching your scenes because

717
00:32:26.220 --> 00:32:30.560
you, I think maybe you and Steve improvised the moSt.

